[s0l0!-header][h1]Functions that Return an Array[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Just as a pointer to an array can be passed into a function, a pointer to an array can be returned, as in the following program: [code format="c" codeId="1713"][b]int *[/b] get_evens();

int main() {
  int *a;
  int k;

  a = get_evens(); /* get first 5 even numbers */
  for (k = 0; k < 5; k++)
    printf("%d\n", a[k]); 

  return 0;
}

[b]int *[/b] get_evens() {
  static int nums[5];
  int k;
  int even = 0;

  for (k = 0; k < 5; k++) {
    nums[k] = even += 2;
  }

  return (nums);
}[/code]
Note that a pointer, not an array, is declared to store the value returned by the function. Also note that when a local variable is being passed out of a function, you need to declare it as [b]static[/b] in the function.

Keep in mind that [b]a[k] [/b]is the same as [b]*(a + k)[/b].
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

